  "explanation": "Template specialization allows programmers to define custom implementations of class or function templates for specific types or conditions. This feature is used to optimize or modify behavior for particular data types, enabling more efficient or tailored code while maintaining generic programming capabilities. It is a core aspect of C++ template metaprogramming, facilitating compile-time customization and type-specific optimizations.\n\nTemplate specialization in C++ allows developers to define custom implementations of a template for specific types or conditions. This feature enables optimized or tailored behavior when the generic template does not suffice, enhancing code flexibility and efficiency in generic programming. It is particularly useful when certain types require specialized handling that differs from the general template logic.\n\nTemplate specialization in C++ allows programmers to define custom implementations of class or function templates for specific template arguments. This feature enables optimized or tailored behavior when the general template implementation is insufficient or inefficient for particular types. It is essential for writing flexible, type-specific code while maintaining the benefits of generic programming, especially in modern C++ where templates are heavily used for generic algorithms and data structures.",
  "syntax": "template <typename T> class ClassName { /* general implementation */ };\ntemplate <> class ClassName<SpecificType> { /* specialized implementation for SpecificType */ };\n\n// For function templates:\ntemplate <typename T> void function(T arg) { /* general implementation */ }\ntemplate <> void function<SpecificType>(SpecificType arg) { /* specialized implementation for SpecificType */ }",
